<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Exodus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Exodus is the founding myth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>, told over four of the five books of the Pentateuch: Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers and Deuteronomy. It tells of the Israelites' bondage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the plagues, their departure through a passage in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> under the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> and the wanderings in the wilderness up to the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>. The story is central in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, recounted daily in prayer and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a>; early Christians read it as a prefiguration of salvation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> recounts it in the life of Moses. Modern scholars hold that the Pentateuch does not give an accurate account of Israelite origins, though some elements may have a historical basis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-exodus.jpg" alt="The Exodus" width="330" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Roberts · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Roberts-IsraelitesLeavingEgypt_1828.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
